A content script’s loads of its extension’s own resources aren’t
subject to the page’s Content Security Policy in Ladybird, by the
URL’s scheme, and the modules it imports are parsed in the world’s
realm and kept in a module map of the world’s own.

Blink and WebKit exempt the scheme the same way. Blink registers the
extension scheme with SchemeRegistry::RegisterURLSchemeAsBypassing-
ContentSecurityPolicy(), and ContentSecurityPolicy::ShouldBypass-
ContentSecurityPolicy() consults SchemeRegistry::SchemeShouldBypass-
ContentSecurityPolicy() for every load. WebKit’s
WebExtensionController::addProcessPool() registers each extension
scheme through
registerURLSchemeAsBypassingContentSecurityPolicy(), and the
ContentSecurityPolicy::allow*FromSource() checks return early on
LegacySchemeRegistry::schemeShouldBypassContentSecurityPolicy(). Gecko
reaches the same outcome by principal rather than scheme: a content
script’s loads carry its expanded principal’s policy, so
nsCSPService::ShouldLoad() consults that CSP instead of the page’s.

Module maps are per world in Blink and Gecko too. Blink’s
Modulator::From() keeps a DocumentModulatorImpl per ScriptState, so an
isolated world has module records of its own, and Gecko’s
SandboxPrivate::GetModuleLoader() gives each content-script sandbox a
ModuleLoader of kind WebExtension, registered with the document’s
ScriptLoader. WebKit keeps one ScriptModuleLoader per Document and
tells the worlds apart by the global object (currentWorld() in
ScriptModuleLoader::fetch()), so its content scripts share the
document’s loader; Ladybird keeps the maps apart, as Blink and Gecko
do.
